{t:"4. The revelation: “one-who-became-a-woman” (pp. 138–139)",p:"pp. 138–139",n:`<ul>
<li>One evening the author asked the man beside him if Ukà was <b>married</b>, since she came with her <b>brother</b> and her <b>name didn't indicate any children</b> (Teduray names reflect children).</li>
<li>Answer: <mark>“Oh no, she can't be married. How could she have children?”</mark> She is <b><mark>mentefuwaley libun</mark></b>, meaning <mark>one-who-became-a-woman</mark>.</li>
<li>Author: “So she is really a man?” Reply: <mark>“No, she is a genuine woman!”</mark> “Genuine” = <b><mark><i>tentu</i></mark></b>, meaning “real” or “actual.”</li>
<li>Author confused: <mark>if she were really a woman, what did it mean that she <i>became</i> one?</mark> (Conversation was in Teduray, <b>with no pronouns</b> like he/she.)</li>
<li>Asked if she was a boy or girl at birth: “She was born a boy. Don't you remember?”</li>
<li>Author (“the dense stranger”): “She is <i>really</i> a man, just dressed like a woman!” Friend: “She is <i>really</i> a woman! She is one-who-became-a-woman.”</li>
<li>Author's <b>“trump card”</b>: “Does she have a penis?” Answer: <b>“Yes, of course she has a penis. She is one-who-became-a-woman.”</b></li></ul>`,
