{t:"5. How the Teduray define gender (p. 139)",p:"p. 139",n:`<ul>
<li>In the author's world, what makes a man “really” male and a woman “really” female is <mark>their genitals</mark>; <mark>evidently not so for the Teduray</mark>.</li>
<li>After asking many people over the following months, he learned that what made you really a certain gender was <mark>the social role you played</mark>: <b>how you dressed, wore your hair, what you did all day, how others addressed you, and what gender you thought of yourself as being</mark></b>.</li>
<li>As far as Teduray were concerned, <mark>you could be whichever one you pleased</mark>.</li>
<li>He later met <b>a man who had been born a girl</b>, chose to be male, and lived a long life as a man.</li>
<li>Most boys grew up wanting to be men and most girls women, but if anyone wanted to switch, <mark>nobody cared a whit</mark>. They were <mark>not thought of as strange or eccentric</mark> and, <mark>except that marriage was considered inappropriate</mark>, were treated like everyone else.</li></ul>`,
