{t:"7. Anthropology and why the Teduray allowed it (p. 140)",p:"p. 140",n:`<ul>
<li>The willingness to let people decide their own gender <b>took the author completely by surprise</b>.</li>
<li>The notion that <mark>gender is a matter of social and cultural definition</mark>, not a biological fact, began to be discussed by <b>feminist anthropologists in the mid-1970s</b>; today it is <b>commonplace</b> in anthropology and among feminist women and men.</li>
<li>In every society, “male” or “female” is a question of <b>who is understood to be what and for what reason</b>, a piece of a society's take on reality, rather than a straightforward question of anatomy.</li>
<li>He met Ukà <b>almost a decade earlier</b> and had never heard of such ideas; had he, he'd have been <b>much less naive</b>.</li>
<li>Through persistent inquiry he reached conclusions: <mark>the Teduray could casually and happily allow people to choose their own gender because being a man carried no higher status than being a woman; men had no power to protect and defend.</mark></li></ul>`,
