{t:"8. Features of Teduray life behind the freedom (pp. 140–141)",p:"pp. 140–141",n:`<ul>
<li>At first, at the festival, the attitude struck him as <b>truly odd</b>. After discussing it with many people over months and thinking analytically, <mark>the notion of freedom to change genders began to seem quite logical</mark>.</li>
<li>Features of Teduray life: <b>very positive attitude toward erotic pleasure</b>; <b>concern for families as the context for economic viability</b>; <b>strong commitment to bearing and raising children</b>; the often-stated belief that gender-changers were <b>excluded from and irrelevant to marriage because they were irrelevant to procreation</b>; and <b>total absence of gender politics</b>.</li>
<li>Together these <mark>provided a social and cultural climate in which anxious concern about gender role-switching had no need or reason to exist</mark>.</li></ul>`,
