{t:"9. Sexuality and language (pp. 141–142)",p:"pp. 141–142",n:`<ul>
<li>In the author's culture, transvestites and transgendered people were <b>sometimes homosexual and sometimes not</b>; he wondered about Teduray ones-who-became-women/men.</li>
<li>Ukà had a family because she was <b>part of her brother's pot</b> (household). <mark>But did her choice mean she was cut off from physical love?</mark> He asked, <mark>with less success</mark>.</li>
<li>He concluded that since gender-changers were irrelevant to conceiving children and didn't marry, and surely wouldn't be denied love partners, <mark>Ukà probably had sex with men</mark>.</li>
<li>Several attempts to ask about sexuality: <mark>met with a complete lack of interest</mark>. He thought he might not know how to ask.</li>
<li>While doing <b>concentrated, systematic dictionary work with Mer</b>, he found <mark>the Teduray language had no terms for homosexual, heterosexual, or bisexual</mark>. They knew the categories existed in <b>other Philippine languages</b> but, <mark>as with distinguishing spouses or siblings by gender</mark>, had apparently never found them useful or necessary.</li>
<li>He didn't think to study homoerotic behavior in Figel; like gender, he hadn't been sensitized to it in training. <mark>He never learned whether women had sexual relations with women, or men with men.</mark> He thinks they did, and would bet <mark>people considered erotic pleasure between persons of the same sex fully acceptable</mark>.</li></ul>`,
