{t:"10. Chapter 10: Cebu Interlude (p. 143)",p:"p. 143",n:`<ul>
<li>The author and his family spent <b>Christmas of 1966 in Cebu City</b>, in the <b>Visayas</b> (central islands of the Philippines).</li>
<li>Tree-lined streets, <b>old Spanish colonial homes</b> of the Filipino <b>urban rich</b> and <b>largely Chinese middle class</b>, plus <b>consulates</b>. Many poorer homes too, but not dominating as in <b>Cotabato and Upi</b>.</li>
<li>Interesting restaurants with good food, a change from <b>Mariano's Chinese place</b> in Cotabato. It felt good to be in a <b>“real city”</b> again.</li>
<li>Invited to spend Christmas with American friends <b>Frazier and Susie Meade</b> and their children. <b>Frazier was the American consul</b> in Cebu City.</li>
<li>He met Frazier at the <b>University of Michigan in Ann Arbor</b> while preparing for Teduray research. Frazier: <b>career diplomat</b>, sent by the <b>State Department</b> for orientation to Philippine history and government.</li>
<li>They met at the home of <b>David Steinberg</b>, a historian specializing in <b>Philippine-American relations during World War II</b>; both were doing independent studies with him.</li></ul>`,
